<p style="color: var(--color-text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem;">
By <a href="/en/urban-gavelin/" rel="author">Urban Gavelin</a>, <time datetime="2026-09-19">September 19, 2026</time>, Personal Development &amp; AI Mindset
</p>
<h1>It's Not AI That's the Robot. It's You.</h1>
<img src={coverImage} alt="It's Not AI That's the Robot. It's You." width="1280" height="720" class="hero-img" />
<p class="lead"><strong>We talk a lot about robots right now. How intelligent they are becoming, which jobs they will take, and what the next AI model will be able to do that the last one could not. But what if we are looking in the wrong direction? What if the truly interesting robot is you. And me.</strong></p>

<div class="section">
<h2>You've Been Under Programming Your Entire Life</h2>
<p>Looked at bluntly, the similarities between how we humans function and how machines function are almost unsettling. We are programmed. We have skills, connectors and plug-ins. We even have an operating system that occasionally needs a restart. For me, coffee tends to do the job reasonably well.</p><p>From the moment you were born, the programming began. Parents, school, friends, teachers, bosses, successes and failures have all fed you information about how the world works and who you are in it.</p><p>Some of that programming has been fantastic. Other code probably should have been updated a long time ago. And yet we keep running it. 'This is how we've always done it.' 'I'm not a tech person.' 'I'm bad with numbers.' 'AI is for the younger people to worry about.' Old programs still running, even though the world around us just received a massive update.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>You Also Have Skills, Connectors, and Plug-ins</h2>
<p>You have skills. You can write, calculate, sell, analyze, lead and solve problems. You have connectors, meaning people who link you to knowledge, experiences and perspectives you don't have on your own.</p><p>And you have plug-ins. Your phone, your CRM system, search engines, and these days, AI. Tools that amplify what you can already do.</p><p>Yet we seem far more preoccupied with tracking AI's updates than our own. What can AI do now? Which model is best? Can it write, analyze and reason better than before? Good questions. But here is perhaps a more important one: what can you do today that you couldn't do a year ago?</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>AI's Development Raises the Bar for You</h2>
<p>If AI can write, you still need to be able to judge whether the text is good. If AI can calculate, you need to understand whether the answer is reasonable. If AI can argue, you need to be able to spot when the argument doesn't hold up. And when AI can give you a thousand answers, you need to get even better at asking the right questions.</p><p>That's why I don't believe the future requires us to think less. On the contrary. We need to train our ability to reason, write, calculate, think critically about sources, and understand other people.</p><p>We need to learn how to think alongside the machine without handing our thinking over to it.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>When Did You Last Update Yourself?</h2>
<p>AI will keep evolving. The interesting question is whether you do too. Which skills do you need to learn? Which old programming needs to be rewritten? Which people do you need to connect with, and which human abilities do you need to train even harder?</p><p>Learn AI, absolutely. But also keep reading, writing, calculating, reasoning, questioning and listening.</p><p>Because the most important question of the future might not be: how intelligent will AI become? It might instead be: how much better are you willing to become at using your own intelligence? AI gets its updates constantly. Make sure you get yours too.</p>
</div>

<div class="section">
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<div itemscope itemtype="https://schema.org/FAQPage">

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
<h3 itemprop="name">What does Urban Gavelin mean by comparing humans to robots?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<p itemprop="text">He is pointing out that humans, much like machines, run on programming shaped by upbringing, experiences and beliefs, and that we also have skills, connectors and plug-ins similar to a technical system. The comparison is meant to highlight that we need to update our own thinking just as often as we track AI updates.</p>
</div>
</div>

<div itemscope itemprop="mainEntity" itemtype="https://schema.org/Question" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">
<h3 itemprop="name">Why does AI development mean we need to think more, not less?</h3>
<div itemscope itemprop="acceptedAnswer" itemtype="https://schema.org/Answer">
<p itemprop="text">As AI produces more text, calculations and arguments, humans need sharper judgment to evaluate whether that output is accurate, relevant and well reasoned. Instead of outsourcing thinking to AI, we need to strengthen our own reasoning, curiosity and critical thinking so we can work alongside the machine rather than depend on it blindly.</p>
</div>
</div>

</div>
